Thank you for the formula.

But there it just looks like you have one covariate x1 that is
measured at the same time as the data and used directly, and another
covariate x2 that is the average over the past 12 days of another
covariate z. In that formulation,
you'd simply precompute x2 and use it as usual.

What other model do you have in mind that would require something else?

Finn

On Mon, 9 Dec 2024 at 21:29, Erin B <erinb...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Sorry for the confusion - in a time period of ~6 years, I have an observed measurement every 12 days, so yes I would say it is instantaneous as although it will account for any accumulation over the last 12 days it is not the average representation.
> I have tried to formulate the observations, a covariate “on the same timescale” and the daily scale covariate by representing the simple model idea (averaging over the gap) in the attached image - I hope this helps clear up my ramblings!
> Many thanks,
> Erin
>
> On Monday, December 9, 2024 at 7:46:04 PM UTC Finn Lindgren wrote:
>>
>> When you say “recorded in a time period every 12th day”, what does that mean? Is it the instantaneous value on each 12th day, or is it the average across a 12 day long period, or is it something else? And what does it mean for a covariate to be available on that scale and another available on a daily scale? It sounds like it’s not the daily time scale covariate that’s the issue here, but rather how the _other_ covariates and the observations themselves behave. Can your write down a formula for all the variables and how they are related, including precise notation for the time scale that clearly indicates what values are aggregated across time and which ones are instantaneous values (or daily values, treating time as discrete on a daily scale)? (The observations, a covariate “on the same timescale” and the daily scale covariate should be sufficient to show the principle)
>> Finn
>>
>> On 9 Dec 2024, at 18:58, Erin B <erinb...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> ﻿I am modelling a continuous variable in space and time, using an SPDE for the spatial effect and an AR1 for the temporal. The observations are recorded in a time period (~2000 time IDs) in a region (~20000 spatial IDs) every 12th day. The rest of the covariates are at the same spatial-temporal scale as the observations, except for this covariate which is in the same time period and region, but recorded every day. This covariate I would like to be non-linear/as a smooth function in the way it links to the observations.
>>
>> Best,
>> Erin
>>
>> On Monday, December 9, 2024 at 5:47:46 PM UTC Finn Lindgren wrote:
>>>
>>> But what is the model you want? _Precisely_ how do you want to link the covariate to the observations? Regardless of implementation details. It’s impossible to give an accurate answer without a precise description of the model.
>>> Finn
>>>
>>> On 9 Dec 2024, at 17:33, Erin B <erinb...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>
>>> ﻿
>>>
>>> I see - I have tried the simplest model (average over the gap) so I was just looking for other alternatives on how to include the covariate as finely as possible (so this is the model I am looking for) - I'm afraid I don't know how to do this.
>>> Thanks,
>>> Erin
>>> On Monday, December 9, 2024 at 4:51:22 PM UTC Finn Lindgren wrote:
>>>>
>>>> Again, it depends on the mechanism, and how realistic a model you’re trying to make. The simplest model would just average the covariate across the observation gap, but that might be completely the wrong mechanism. This is a modelling and/or research question; I may be able to answer questions about how to implement a specific such model, but it’s really up to you to figure out what model it is you need/want.
>>>> Finn
>>>>
>>>> On 9 Dec 2024, at 16:48, Erin B <erinb...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>>
>>>> ﻿Hi Finn,
>>>>
>>>> Thank you for your quick response. The covariate has an aggregate effect. How could one build a model for how it links to the observations?
>>>> Best,
>>>> Erin
>>>>
>>>> On Thursday, December 5, 2024 at 10:04:55 PM UTC Finn Lindgren wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>> This depends on whether the covariate has an instantaneous effect (in which case the finer resolution is only useful for interpolation/prediction) or some kind of aggregate effect (in which case one might be able to build a model for how it links to the observations). So one would need to understand the mechanisms behind the observations to be able to give an appropriate answer in any particular model/data case.
>>>>> Finn
>>>>>
>>>>> On 5 Dec 2024, at 13:28, Erin B <erinb...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>> ﻿Hi all,
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>> I am fitting a space-time model in INLA with an SPDE and have available data at a finer temporal resolution for one of my covariates (data is measured every 12 days in a region and this covariate is measured every day in the same region). I would like to know if I can include this finer covariate data in some (functional?) way?
>>>>>
>>>>> Many thanks,
>>>>> Erin
>>>>>
